                        "entry_id": "67862bfe-f81a-359d-9d7f-112f1ba9d760",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A solstice."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncommon"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1cbc4ed4-f777-42c7-95bc-7c28772445e2",
                                "sense_id": "bdf298bb-f70a-348c-9b85-76005fbe01a0",
                                "text": "Now was it the season of the yeer past sun-stead in summer and neer unto the houre of noon-stead in the day, so as they journeyed in a way full of dust, when the sun was exceeding hot; and even now they began to feel thirst and weariness already."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "eef40a2e-b312-4092-9b14-6b4122a0f88b",
                                "sense_id": "bdf298bb-f70a-348c-9b85-76005fbe01a0",
                                "text": "The summer-sunnestead falleth out alwaies [in Italie] to be just upon the foure and twentie day of June, at what time as the sunne is entred eight degrees within Cancer."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ee8bcf35-7d76-4851-a03a-4f315b0de54c",
                                "sense_id": "bdf298bb-f70a-348c-9b85-76005fbe01a0",
                                "text": "Sun-ſteads or Tropicks"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a7e96ddf-fbe1-4deb-8fd2-2b20c29ecf36",
                                "sense_id": "bdf298bb-f70a-348c-9b85-76005fbe01a0",
                                "text": "The Summer Solstice is the central point of the tropic (trepo, to turn), which begins upon the festival of Barnaby bright, and continues for three weeks. […] According to Olympiodorus, an Egyptian of the fifth century, the rising of the Dog-star was just twenty days after the estival \"sunsted.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f4983b67-176c-4af6-bf68-c84f9bfa8afb",
                                "sense_id": "bdf298bb-f70a-348c-9b85-76005fbe01a0",
                                "text": "Sun-stead = solstice."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "580c5331-9fba-4586-8a7e-5d209d7ee6b4",
                                "sense_id": "bdf298bb-f70a-348c-9b85-76005fbe01a0",
                                "text": "Sirius in the mouth of the watch dog vied in the dignity and importance of the significance of its ascent with the midnight appearance of Vindemiatrix in the left elbow of the headless virgin, on the horizon at the winter \"sunstead\" of six months later."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "eb5b048e-a29c-444b-b1fd-aba35692593d",
                                "sense_id": "bdf298bb-f70a-348c-9b85-76005fbe01a0",
                                "text": "Solitary Winter Sunstead and Yule (December 21) If a follower of the Old Way cannot find others of like mind to share a seasonal ritual in honor of High Valhalla, this rite may be performed by the solitary individual […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
